  "ai_overview": {
    "text_blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "In Utah, homeowners insurance typically covers a roof leak only if it results from a sudden, accidental, covered peril\u2014such as wind, hail, lightning, falling trees/objects, or collapse from ice or snow\u2014minus your deductible. Leaks caused by age, normal wear and tear, lack of maintenance, rot, or improper installation are generally excluded.",
        "reference_indexes": [
          0,
          1,
          2,
          3
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "What Is Typically Covered"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Storm and weather damage: Leaks caused by hail, heavy wind, wind-driven rain, or heavy snow/ice collapse.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              1,
              2
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Interior and personal property damage: Resulting water damage to ceilings, walls, or belongings when a covered peril damages the roof.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              3,
              4
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Temporary mitigation: Reasonable steps to stop active water intrusion, such as tarping the roof (save receipts).",
            "reference_indexes": [
              2,
              5
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "What Is Usually Not Covered"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Gradual wear and maintenance: Old shingles, worn flashing, rotting materials, or neglected upkeep.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              1,
              3,
              6
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Interior water without roof/wall damage: Storm-driven water entering when the roof or walls were not damaged by the storm, as well as flooding or sewer backups (unless covered by separate endorsements).",
            "reference_indexes": [
              2
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Certain policy limits/exclusions: Some policies apply Actual Cash Value (ACV) depreciation on older roofs rather than Replacement Cost Value (RCV), limit cosmetic damage, or carry separate wind/hail deductibles.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              5,
              6
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Key Utah Claim Rules & Next Steps"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Prevent further damage and document: Take photos/video of roof and interior damage, tarp active leaks if safe, keep receipts, and avoid permanent repairs until your insurer inspects and you agree on repair costs.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              2
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Utah claims timeline: Under Utah rules, insurers generally must acknowledge your claim within 15 days and accept, deny, or explain why more time is needed within 30 days after receiving a complete proof of loss.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              7
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Official guidance: Review your policy declarations page and check the Utah Insurance Department for state claim guidelines and consumer resources.",
            "snippet_links": [
              {
                "text": "Utah Insurance Department",
                "link": "https://insurance.utah.gov/consumers/home-insurance/home-claims-info/"
              }
            ],
            "reference_indexes": [
              2,
              5
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "If you share what caused the leak (such as a recent storm or gradual wear), your roof's approximate age , and whether you have interior water damage, I can help you evaluate whether filing a claim makes sense."
      }
    ],
